{% extends 'base.html.j2' %}
{% import 'partials.html.j2' as partials with context %}
{% set title = "Methods | " ~ site.name %}
{% set description = "How each reading is made, from the dock to the band call: collection, Coliscan Easygel analysis, the criteria, and the limits of a one-station field method." %}

{% block main %}<div class="grid">
<div class="col-main">
<span class="kicker">Station {{ station.id }}</span>
<h1>Methods</h1>
<p class="lede">How each number is made, from the dock to the band call, and what the method cannot tell you.</p>

<h2 id="site">Site</h2>
<p>One fixed station, {{ station.id }}, the Nereid Boat Club dock in Rutherford, New Jersey, on the tidal Lower Passaic below the Dundee Dam. The published coordinate is rounded to two decimal places on purpose: one person stands in the same place every week. Measured along the river, the dock is at about river mile 12, inside the upper nine miles of the Lower Passaic covered by EPA's interim sediment cleanup plan.</p>

<h2 id="collection">Collection</h2>
<p>Grab sample from the dock. A sterile bottle is opened about 30 cm below the surface with the mouth facing into the current and the arm extended upstream, capped promptly, dried, labeled with the week and the exact clock time, and put on ice. Plating happens at home inside the holding time.</p>
<p>Sampling began 23 August 2026. The regular window is {{ station.sampling_window }}; the exact time of every sample is in the published record.</p>

<h2 id="analysis">Analysis</h2>
<p>Coliscan Easygel: 5 mL of sample per plate, poured in duplicate, incubated 24 hours at 35 C. Purple and dark blue colonies are counted as E. coli. Pink, red and magenta colonies are other coliforms &mdash; recorded, not counted as E. coli. Teal, blue-green, white and tan colonies are not counted. The reported value is the mean of the two duplicate counts divided by the plated volume, times 100, giving CFU per 100 mL. At the standard 5 mL that is a multiplier of 20.</p>
<p>Weeks 1 and 2 were plated with more than the nominal 5 mL: the transfer-pipette draws were overfilled, and a later calibration against a marked test tube measured the real volume at about 8.3 mL per plate. Those two records use the measured volume (a multiplier of 12), and each carries the full correction in its comments. From week 3 on, draws stop at the pipette's graduation and the volume is nominal.</p>
<p>Uncountable plates are recorded as uncountable, never estimated. Plates are photographed beside their week label before disposal, and disposal is a 20 minute bleach soak in a sealed bag.</p>

<h2 id="parameters">Field parameters</h2>
<p>Recorded on the dock with each sample: water temperature, pH by field strip, turbidity as depth of visibility in a transparency tube (reported in cm; higher is clearer), nitrate as N and orthophosphate by field test strips, and tide stage.</p>
<p>Antecedent rainfall, the 48 hour total before the sample, is the input the advisory most wants. A project rain gauge is now mounted at the boathouse and read and reset regularly, so most records carry its figure directly. The first two weeks predate the gauge and are attributed to the nearest airport weather station instead; every record names its rain source.</p>

<h2 id="criteria">The criteria, and which one each call uses</h2>
<p>Readings are scored against the EPA recreational water quality values for E. coli in fresh water: the 126 CFU/100 mL geometric mean criterion and the 235 CFU/100 mL single sample value. Each week's band is a single sample call: under {{ criteria.geometric_mean }} is green, {{ criteria.geometric_mean }} to {{ criteria.single_sample }} is yellow, over {{ criteria.single_sample }} is red.</p>
<p>Those are the primary contact recreation values. NJDEP treats tidal water as fresh (FW2), and designated for primary contact recreation, while salinity stays at or below 3.5 parts per thousand at mean high tide. Published salt front studies put the fresh-to-saline boundary several miles below the Dundee Dam, so the Nereid dock is very likely in the fresh stretch. That is an inference from published research, not yet confirmed against NJDEP's segment map for this exact point.</p>

<h2 id="qc">Quality control</h2>
<p>Duplicate plates every week, both counts published. The first laboratory split, from week 2, has come back from Pace Analytical Services, LLC-Fairfield NJ (NELAP/TNI accredited, NJ certification 07010), with the certificate of analysis and chain-of-custody on file: greater than 2,420 MPN/100 mL by Colilert-18, flagged too numerous to count at the Quanti-Tray/2000's quantification limit, against this project's 1,620 CFU/100 mL from the same bottle &mdash; at least a 39.6% relative percent difference and at least 0.17 log10 apart, since the true lab count is higher than the number it was flagged at. The two methods do not agree closely, but they agree on what matters most: both call the water red. More splits are planned through the season to see whether that gap holds up, narrows, or was one sample's noise.</p>

<h2 id="union-ave">Comparison with the nearest professional station</h2>
<p>The nearest long-term monitoring station is the Passaic River at Union Ave, about 1.2 km upstream of the dock along the river. The New Jersey Harbor Dischargers Group ran it from 2011 to 2019, and since 2021 its results have been published under the Interstate Environmental Commission. It is sampled from the bridge, mid-stream, by certified-laboratory membrane filtration (EPA Method 1603, then 1103.1), and its results reach the public <a class='tx' href='https://www.waterqualitydata.us/data/Result/search?siteid=IEC-NYNJCT-NJHDG_7&amp;siteid=NJHDG-7&amp;characteristicName=Escherichia%20coli'>Water Quality Portal</a> about five to seven weeks after sampling. As of 19 October 2026 its latest published sample is from 27 August, so no week of this season can yet be compared day by day.</p>
<p><strong>E. coli.</strong> Between 20 August and 5 October in the years 2011 to 2025, the station has 41 sampling days, with a geometric mean of 1,310 CFU/100 mL. (This excludes results the portal flags for quality-control problems.) This season's {{ word(stats.n) }} readings here have a geometric mean of {{ commas(stats.season_gm) }}, and they fall across the station's range rather than at one end of it. With so few samples, the difference is not statistically meaningful. The fair claim is that the readings here are consistent with what a certified laboratory has measured nearby, not that they are the same. Possible reasons the readings here could run lower, none of them tested:</p>
<ul>
<li>most samples here are taken in the early evening after a day of sunlight, which kills bacteria near the surface, while the station is sampled around 7:30 to 9 in the morning;</li>
<li>Easygel may undercount; the one laboratory split so far came back higher than the plate count (see <a class='tx' href='#qc'>quality control</a>);</li>
<li>the dock is at the river's edge, while the station samples mid-stream, about 1.2 km upstream.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>pH and nitrate are approximate.</strong> In late August the field pH here read 7.1 to 7.2, while the station's laboratory pH was 7.49 to 7.56 on nearby days. The field nitrate test has read 1.5 mg/L every week, while the station measured nitrate plus nitrite at 0.69 mg/L on 13 August. Treat these two parameters as rough field readings. No advisory or conclusion on this site rests on them.</p>
<p><strong>Temperature and turbidity.</strong> Water temperature agrees closely with the station. Turbidity here is a transparency-tube depth in cm, and the station's is in NTU, so the two cannot be compared directly.</p>

<h2 id="limits">What this cannot tell you</h2>
<p>One station, one sampling slot a week (Sunday early evening, with laboratory split weeks sampled on a weekday morning instead), a field method rather than a laboratory method, and a short record so far. This is not a characterization of the Passaic River. It is a characterization of the water at one dock at the hours and on the days it was sampled, published with every number needed to check it.</p>
<p>The 48 hour rainfall figure misses storms that end just before its window. That happened before weeks 1, 2 and 3, and most clearly before week 6: the gauge logged 2.51 in from 27 to 29 September, but the 2 October sample has a 48 hour figure of 0.00 in and counts as a dry week. The full rain gauge log is on the <a class='tx' href='/data/'>data page</a> so storms like that stay visible.</p>
